   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to shellfish\nregulations",
    "basePrintNo": "S6363",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6363-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0319, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6363\n\nSPONSOR: MARTINEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to shellfish regulations\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo amend Section 13-0319 of the Environmental Conservation Law to speci-\nfy the i nclusion of scallops as in accordance with section 13-0327 of\nthe Environmental Conservation Law as a species of mollusks subject to\nthe laws of permitting and transportation of shellfish pursuant to\nsection 13-0319 of the Environmental Conservation Law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill is intended to amend Section 13-0319 of the Environmental\nConservation Law so as to include scallops as a class of aquatic species\nsubject the same rules of permitting and transportation as any other\nshellfish species.   \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF\nAPPLICABLE):\n\nThe inclusion of the following language \"transplanting of scallops\npursuant to (paragraph a of subdivision 2 of) section 13-0327\" in subdi-\nvision 1 of section 13-0319 of the Environmental Conservation Law.\"\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nECL § 13-0319 is the overall enabling statute which empowers the DEC to\nregulate shellfish, including but not limited to the authority to regu-\nlate and administer through permitting. State attempts to control\nshellfish quality have been plagued by a lack of uniform authority. The\nimportance of regulating and monitoring both the shellfish population as\nwell the quality of the shellfish are symbiotic and must have a standard\nby which same can be achieved. Shellfish harvested from polluted waters\npose a significant health threat to consumers as well as placing the\nshellfish population in the cross sight of endangerment if not\nextinction from local waters. Utilizing the term \"shellfish\" in its\ncolloquial vernacular; scallops a species of mollusks inadvertently not\nincluded within section ECL § 13-0319, and although a bivalve species of\nmollusk; scallops improperly harvested and or transported can pose a\nsignificant danger and health hazard, as such it is imperative to\ninclude scallops with section ECL § 13-0319.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR SAND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nIf any to be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
